<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Xochimilco</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1430 CE (approx.) · capital Xochimilco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Nahua altepetl on the southern lakes of the Valley of Mexico, famed for its chinampa gardens, conquered by the Mexica around 1430.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Xochimilca were one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> groups said to have migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztlan">Aztlan</a> and settled in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> in the 12th or 13th century. They established Xochimilco on the shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-xochimilco">Lake Xochimilco</a> around 1200.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Xochimilco was ruled by its own tlatoque and controlled a domain stretching into the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a>. Its people were masters of chinampa farming, building raised fields of mud and vegetation in the shallow lake, which produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, vegetables and flowers in abundance. Their stonework and gardening skill were valued by neighbouring cities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tepanec-empire">Tepanec Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> forces conquered Xochimilco around 1430 and made it a tributary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, using its labour to build the causeway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinampa">chinampas</a> survive and are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/xochimilco.jpg" alt="Xochimilco" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: ProtoplasmaKid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trajinera_en_canal_Nativitas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1430 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xochimilco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nahuatl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xochimilca</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1315&amp;focus=xochimilco" aria-label="Xochimilco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2001 1464 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Xochimilco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2321 1720L2322 1714"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2321 1720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2321 1720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1315&amp;focus=xochimilco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

